1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Two fair coins. A is a head on the first, B a head on the second. Four outcomes, each one quarter.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:15,000
A holds two of the four cells, so its probability is one half. B holds two cells as well.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:23,000
They overlap in one cell. One quarter equals one half times one half, so A and B are independent.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
The product rule is the definition, not a consequence. It is checked, never assumed from a picture.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
With three events the definition multiplies every subset of size two or more. That is four conditions.

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Pairwise independence means the three pair conditions hold. It says nothing about the triple.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:52,000
For n events the count is two to the n, minus n, minus one. Two events need one check, three need four, six need fifty-seven.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Mutual independence is the full list. A question that says only independent means mutual, and it means all of them.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Four tickets, each a three-digit label: 112, 121, 211 and 222. One is drawn uniformly.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
A1 is a one in the first place: tickets 112 and 121. A2 is a one in the second place, A3 in the third.

11
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Every pair meets in exactly one ticket. One quarter against one half times one half: all three pairs pass.

12
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
All three together would need a one in every place. No ticket has three ones, so the triple intersection is empty.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,000
The failure is the whole lesson: three passing pair tests say nothing about the three together.

14
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Two dice, thirty-six equally likely cells. A is a first die of 1, 2 or 3. B is a first die of 3, 4 or 5.

15
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:50,000
C is the event that the faces sum to nine: four cells. Its probability is one ninth.

16
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:58,000
A with B is six cells, one sixth, not one quarter. A with C is one cell, one thirty-sixth, not one eighteenth.

17
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Yet all three together is one cell, three six, and one thirty-sixth equals one half times one half times one ninth.

18
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Two spaces, two failures. The conditions are independent tests, and both directions of the mistake are real.

19
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Two fair bits, X and Y, and a third bit Z defined as one when X and Y differ.

20
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Four rows: 000, 011, 101, 110. Each of the three bits is one in exactly two rows.

21
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Every pair shares two rows out of four. One quarter equals one half times one half, so all three pairs pass again.

22
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
But all three together is never 111, so the triple is zero against one eighth. Any two bits determine the third.

23
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:44,000
The at-least-one formula multiplies the complements: one minus the product of one minus each probability.

24
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:52,000
On the four tickets the formula gives seven eighths. The truth is three quarters, because 222 is the only ticket outside the union.

25
00:02:52,000 --> 00:03:00,000
On the dice the formula gives twenty-eight thirty-sixths. The truth is thirty-one thirty-sixths, so here it is too small.

26
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
With mutual independence the formula is exact, and that is the practical reason the distinction matters.

27
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Now condition on Z. Keep only the rows where Z is one: 011 and 101.

28
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Inside those rows X is one half and Y is one half. But X and Y together never happens.

29
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
So X and Y are independent, and dependent once Z is known. Conditional independence is a separate statement.

30
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
This is the shape of explaining-away arguments, and of every question that says given.

31
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:44,000
Disjoint is not independent. Two events that cannot both happen are dependent, unless one has probability zero.

32
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:51,000
An impossible event and a certain event are independent of everything, including themselves.

33
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:58,000
Taking the complement preserves independence: P of A with not B is P of A minus P of A with B.

34
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:04,000
So complements are free, disjointness is not, and zero or one is a boundary rather than a rule.

35
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:12,000
A four-point space has sixteen events. Two of them are independent of every other event.

36
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Eighty-four of the two hundred and fifty-six ordered pairs of events are independent pairs.

37
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Six two-element events give twenty triples. Eight of them are pairwise independent and not mutually independent.

38
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:36,000
On a small space every one of these can be counted by hand. That is the safest way to answer a counting question about independence.

39
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:44,000
GATE CSE 2023: two coins. A is a head on both throws, B a head on the first, C a head on the second.

40
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:52,000
A is one cell, B and C are two each. A with B is one quarter against one eighth, so A with B fails.

41
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:59,000
B with C is the same single cell, one quarter, and one half times one half is one quarter. That pair passes.

42
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:06,000
The true statements are B and C independent, and the conditional equality. Both are the same arithmetic.

43
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:15,000
Three places use it: reliability of components, at-least-one over repeated trials, and the binomial count.

44
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:23,000
The four-step check: how many events, every subset of size two or more, both sides of each product, and no inference from a picture.

45
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:30,000
Independent means the whole list. Pairwise means three of the four, and a question will offer it as a distractor.

